WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.469
-Whoo! Hi!

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.705
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you!

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.173
Please, sit down.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.908
You don't know
how the set's gonna go.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:09.976
[ Laughter ]

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.345
Whoo! New York City!
We back open!

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.047
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.614
Wow! Man!

00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:18.684
I went out last night, and
I've been hungover,

00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:20.753
uh, for years.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:23.089
[ Laughs ]
Here's a little secret.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.124
When I was 13, growing up
in Puerto Rico,

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.592
I almost drowned.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:27.760
Don't worry, I made it.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.263
But that's
the second scariest thing

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.198
that's ever happened to me,
because, last week,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.234
a stranger got in
a revolving door with me.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.402
[ Laughter ]

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.204
I'm sorry.
If you're not laughing,

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:39.405
I assume you're a terrorist,

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.340
because that's not what we do
in this country.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.744
It was a circular one,
like the four-corner one,

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.647
and I walked in real confident
like I'd used it before.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.183
And midway through, it jammed.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.753
And I heard a voice
that was not mine.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.321
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.557
I heard a voice go,
"What's the holdup?"

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:04.230
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.798
I was just like,
"Miss, you're the holdup.

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:07.733
Who let you in here?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.735
It's a one-person wedge.
We all learned this.

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:12.237
It's real basic, and
the lady got real nervous,

00:01:12.237 --> 00:01:13.405
because she turned around

00:01:13.405 --> 00:01:15.575
and tried to push the door
the other way.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.610
But that's not how
those doors work, is it?

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.711
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.246
So I had to like,
"Hey, [Smooching] focus."

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:22.381
Like, Gather back in the right."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.381 --> 00:01:25.251
But to anybody who saw us,
we just looked like

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:29.656
a couple who needed a moment,
like we were arguing

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:32.325
and this was the only
quiet place we could find.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:37.663
Man, I caught my first flight
since B-CVD,

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:40.900
and people still flying tight.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.969
Like, people are flying
like our parents used to fly.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.203
Like, efficient.

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:46.206
Like, nobody's just, like,
flying for funsies.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:47.906
Like, you better have a purpose.

00:01:47.906 --> 00:01:50.509
Everybody boarded quickly,
nobody made eye contact.

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:51.778
They were just like,
"Fauci didn't say

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.479
we could get it through the eye,
but what do I know?

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.083
So just, like,
buckle up and let's go!"

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.285
Like that.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.154
I didn't think
we were gonna make it,

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.722
'cause the dude flying the plane

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:04.924
was walking around the terminal,
slouching.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.358
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:06.358 --> 00:02:09.261
I know he's called a pilot,
but he didn't have pilot energy.

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.430
You know what I mean?
That's a cool job.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.099
Pop your chest out,
believe in yourself.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.433
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.803
The dude was just walking around
just like,

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.738
"I guess we'll try this again,"
like that.

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:19.772
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.542
Mnh-mnh.
Your carry-on look empty.

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.210
You look like you
ain't trying to land.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.413
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.583
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:36.322
If you're a pilot,
you're better than us.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.790
Show it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.159
That's one of the few jobs
you can brag

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:42.194
and we just have to take it.

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:43.896
It's like pro athletes --

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.865
not golfers,
but, like, the other ones.

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.066
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.501
The people making
Girl Scout cookies

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.003
and pilots --
the only ones we trust.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.304
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.339
The scariest part was,
we were all seated,

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:54.741
everybody had
that nervous energy,

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.075
and right before we pulled back,

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.743
the pilot jumped
on the microphone,

00:02:57.743 --> 00:03:00.380
and he was like, "Um,
this is your captain, Robert."

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.982
I was like, "Um?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:05.618
"Wait a minute,
you open with 'Um'?!"

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:06.886
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.621
"Dude, you can't be hesitant.
Not right now.

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.556
We told you we on edge.

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:14.093
Captain Robert?
Nah, you Bobby."

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.096
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.466
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.004
I did witness real maternal love
on the flight,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.705
which is always dope.
I love that.

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:28.140
I don't have a kid,
but I love

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.575
when a parent,
like, gets defensive

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:31.878
and just, like, hulks up and
just like, "No, I made this one.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.212
Step back."

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:35.414
This is what happened.
I'll never forget it.

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:38.150
I was seated in 26-A,
because I have status.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.519
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:43.255
The dude next to me, 26-C,
not with me.

00:03:43.255 --> 00:03:46.926
Very important, Jimmy.
Uh, baby crying, 24-A.

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.894
Babies cry, and
dude next to me just goes,

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:53.165
"Ugh! I hope this baby
doesn't cry the whole time."

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:55.868
But he said it loud enough

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.504
like he was hoping
that the baby might hear.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:03:59.805
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean?

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:01.440
Like the baby might go,
"Oh, my God, I'm so sorry.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.242
I didn't realize
it was nap-nap time.

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.678
I'll keep it down."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:07.913
Dude said that.
Baby doesn't respond.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.549
But who does respond is
the mother of this baby.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.286
So, 26-C goes, "I hope the baby
doesn't cry the whole time."

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:16.455
And then we just hear
an angelic voice from 24-A

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.791
go, "What mother-flubber,
you don't cry?"

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.826
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.662
And it was
the most logical thing

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.963
I've ever heard
on how to defend a baby.

00:04:24.963 --> 00:04:27.500
[ Laughter ]
People don't like babies crying,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.834
because they get to
do it in public,

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.903
while the rest of us
have to wait for a shower.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.839
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:36.409
[ Applause ]

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.814
I tip my hat to parents
after this year.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.216
This past year, man,
that stress was different.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.018
Just on virtual learning alone.

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:49.788
I've been helping my niece out,
because she recently found out

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:51.991
I only work
15 or 20 minutes a night.

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.259
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.594
She was like, "Oh,
this guy got some time."

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:58.030
And it's stressful,
because before the parent,

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.065
I imagine if your kid
got a bad grade,

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:02.100
you would go,
"Hey, what happened?

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.304
Did you not study?
Who taught you?"

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:08.507
But now this blame go 50/50.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:10.175
So, like, now your kid
get a bad grade,

00:05:10.175 --> 00:05:13.145
you don't have a dumb kid.
"We a dumb family."

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:15.547
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:18.484
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.553
Have a good night, guys.
My name is Rojo Perez.

